e it a step further  so we looked at an ellipsoidal a conical  and a cylindrical electrode all made  using 3d printing  much like this previous example we put  several holes on the side so  we could vary the electrospray emitter  position  uh a lower number so for example on the  right you see the cylinder five  millimeter  corresponds to the nano spray emitter  being closer to the exit  and a larger number means that the  emitter is further back  from the exit you can see that  as we move the emitter further back from  the exit  the ion intensity detected by the ion  ccd  gets lower but the peak gets narrower  if we compare the cone the cylinder and  the ellipse electrodes  we can see that the cone gives us both  the narrowest peak  and the highest intensity five  millimeters from the exit  the cylinder is the second highest and  the ellipse  seems to have the worst performance  so one thing that our group is  interested in is soft landing  or ambient soft landing experiments so  being able to make ion beams  and control their current is really  important to us  and what we can see is that a  cone-shaped electrode  given by the red triangles seems to in  all cases  have a very nice focus and a relatively  high intensity  for the full width half max  however most people would probably be  more interested in  the applications to mass spec that we've  observed with these  electrodes so this is again  from the 2012 paper with an  ellipsoidally shaped electrode  in panel a you can see the effect of  focusing a tune mixture  with this ellipsoidal electrode  as you can see the main peaks are  caffeine the smurfa peptide  and these ultramark series of compounds  without focusing which is panel b  you can see that the mirfa peptide and  several of these smaller peaks around  caffeine  are actually suppressed relative to the  ultra mark  compounds so as i said  at atmospheric pressure we're interested  in mobility as opposed to mass to charge  so this implied that there is s