t see  any of this focusing effect  so it seems like ion generation is key  to getting this focusing  to think about why this works i think  it's helpful  to look at this in a very uh sophomore  physics way  here we have two particles p1 and p2  they're the same polarity and there's  some force between them  which is dependent on one over the  distance squared  in this case i'll call this force f12  so the particle 1 experiences only force  f12 whenever we add in an ion  of opposite polarity we now have two  forces on p1  f12 and the attractive force f13  so we put this all together the net  force is now  f12 minus f13  this means that we're actually  decreasing the force felt on p1  from p2 by adding in this charged  ion and that is why this peak goes from  this broad eight millimeter  thing to you know about two and a half  millimeters or so  we're actually reducing the space charge  in the packet  uh there are obvious applications where  you could say  multiplex nanospray emitters and use  this technique to bring the beams  together to get very high currents  however you could also use this for mass  spectrometry  and our findings in doing that are very  interesting  on the left we have the sltaa with the  apci off  in black and 8pc on and red  and you can see that whenever we turn on  the apci  the intensity is decreased by about 20  percent or so  however whenever we go to a higher mass  ion like the octal taa  shown on the right turning on the apci  actually increases the intensity of that  peak  this is again a complete contrast to  what we saw with the  ellipsoidal and conical electrodes  so we still don't understand this effect  really  but it is highly reproducible  we're somewhat hopeful that we can find  a way to exploit this  to do some interesting applications  again though we don't really understand  the effect yet  we're doing some simulations now with  higher level  physics software to try to understand it  but it's  interesting finding nonetheless  especially because you would expect  lighter ions to be more affected by  space charge  so in summary ions at high pressure  display  wildly different behaviors and ions in a  vacuum environment  our lab has done a number of different  methods of ion manipulation and  atmospheric pressure  we really only ever use dc potentials  the instrumentation is much simpler than  a traditional in vacuum experiment  of course mobility effects can influence  ion transmission  and it seems like the focusing devices  can really play a part in increasing  those effects  maybe we could even possibly use them  for some application  we've shown that space charge can be  managed in these  low energy atmospheric pressure ions  which opens the door to brighter ion  sources in the future  but i should say that we don't really  understand a lot of these phenomena  still  there are a lot of questions especially  about the role that droplets play  while i started talking out about  droplets i  quickly moved on to talking about ions  because a droplet is a highly dynamic  environment  we need to be able to understand what  effect evaporation and ion formation  plays on the focusing and ion  transmission effects that we observe in  these devices  so with that i'd be happy to take your  questions in the live q a session  thanks  thank you both for those great  presentations we're now going to start  the q a portion of the webinar if you  have a question you'd like to ask please  do so now  just click on that ask a question box  that's located on the far  left of your screen i'm going to go  ahead and get started i'll let  the two of you dr batoon and dr marsh  decide who is answering  the first one i have is why did you use  3d printing to fabricate the devices  used in your experiments  yeah so that's a question that we get  quite a bit about this work  um the simple answer is that 3d printing  is really cheap  and in the case of the ion mobility  spectrometer  it's very lightweight that spectrometer  probably weighs about 100 grams  so for a field use case for a device  like that which is more applied it's  really good to have that lightweight but  it's really cheap and you can also make  geometries which are kind of unusual to  get by  normal machining so  you know it's really nice for us too  because we can just go in  dream up something and then an hour or  so we have it in our hand  so we like it for all of those reasons  okay  and then there's another question  related to that  what is this 3d printed material made  out of  are they doped with some kind of  conductive metal  so the plastics that we use here are  in almost all cases pla based  which is probably one of the most common  3d printing materials  in the case of the conductive electrodes  we  have a special carbon doped plastic  which you can buy  anywhere we get it off of amazon we've  also tried plastics which are carbon  nanotube dope  those work fine but they're just a  little bit harder to actually print with  um but in either case though you know we  find that these things  are not nearly as conductive as say an  actual metal  but you know the resistance across one  of these is  probably less than a mega ohm typically  for pretty much any of these devices so  still pretty conductive  okay i'm going to stay on this theme for  just a minute so putting on the spot  again  will the 3d printed part withstand heat  yes that's something we haven't explored  too much  obviously the plastic itself has some  temperature that's printed at  where it does melt and in the case of  the pla  you know we're talking around you know  maybe  mid 200 c range you could probably get  it too before it starts getting really  bad  but we also use petg  plastic which we print with at about 270  or so  and that seems to work fine up to that  range  so there is some heat stability  uh one thing with this ambient ims that  we've thought about  is including a desolvation region like  you would have a typical drift tube ims  but obviously heating this plastic as i  said we can go  reasonably high but probably not high  enough to efficiently desolvate  everything  so there are some drawbacks using the  plastic for sure okay  um switching gears now a little bit this  might be for dr patoon  um you mentioned some bias in ion  transmission  is this mz dependent mass dependent or  collision cross-section dependent  um tough question  in regards to the mass spectrometer  itself we're tuned  to to transmit across the m over z range  so a lot of these  observations are primarily due to the  um ion focusing devices but do you have  any more commentary regarding vm  i guess the you'd call it mass  discrimination  yeah so this is something we don't  really understand yet  as i showed we have you know six or  seven points which  look pretty good um but  the compounds that we picked their mass  and their uh mobility or our collision  cross section are  pretty much in lockstep with each other  so we haven't really done the  experiments to figure this out yet  and in fact we're actually getting back  to the lab this week so  that's one of the first things that uh  sakeb will be doing  i think though that it's almost  certainly a collision cross-section  effect  obviously at atmospheric pressure a lot  of these  mass to charge things are kind of  blanketed by the mobility effects  but i can't say for sure but i'm  extremely confident that it's a mobility  cross-section effect  okay excellent um not sure who this one  should go to  can you comment more on the negative  apci  source used to reduce space charge  what counter ion is used and can reagent  gases be  added to study ion-ion interactions as  well  okay yeah i think that one's for me so  we just used a needle held at  some potential i think in this case it  was at about  5 kb or so to generate a corona  discharge in the open air  so we weren't really selecting for any  certain ions it's just whatever negative  ions are produced by the discharge  and that's also why we use the taa ions  because they're pretty non-reactive so  i'm sure if we use something that was  more reactive we probably would have  seen  these reactive species generated by the  discharge  interacting more i think that to make  this  really useful you do have to take a gas  or some conditions to get rid of these  reactive oxygen and nitrogen species  so yeah i can definitely see an approach  where we  carefully select the gas and look for a  certain interaction  or reaction depending on the sort of  work we're doing  okay great next one is  stacked ring rf ion funnels are in  common use today  can you comment on the differences  between the two technologies  okay i'll take a stab at this okay yeah  sure  sorry brett yeah um in our in one of our  instruments the 6495c  we have an iron final device however  it's in partial vacuum and it is  evacuated at a  a lower pressure mode these devices are  completely atmospheric pressure  and i'm sure brett has a lot more to say  about that  yeah well i mean patrick's pretty much  right i mean if you look at this  ims device that has been developed in  the lab  it is just a set of rings some gradient  on it  but at atmospheric pressure we get  focusing without the need for rf  uh frequencies to be added unlike the  ion funnel  now obviously the focusing that we get  is  pretty good but you still have to go  through the atmospheric pressure inlet  of the mass spec  so no matter what you do i think you're  going to get quite a bit of ion loss  in that ims device and  as patrick said you know we are  operating at completely atmospheric  pressure  no pumping or anything um but this  certainly would not replace an ion  funnel  you know an ion funnel will take  everything that gets into the mass spec  and channel very efficiently  whereas we're entirely outside of the  mass spec  okay um there's two questions that seem  somewhat related i'll ask them together  and you can  decide if they can be answered at once  or not with the material being used in  the 3d printing  do they not present interference or  contamination  in the analysis and then relatedly  someone asked about any background from  leaching from the from the plastic being  used  yeah so i think that these are yeah  very related the short answer is  we don't see any evidence in our mass  spectra that  there's a background from the plastic  itself  i will say that if you run one of these  devices  for a while you can start to see peaks  from things you've previously put into  it  so there is something building up on the  surface but  i think that whatever is in these  plastics  you know whether tg or pla is pretty  stable  it doesn't seem to be extracted  certainly you know these things have  some  volatile chemicals i would assume  that can leech off of them over time but  we don't see any evidence of that  okay excellent um next i was surprised  to see the  increase in the positive ion response  when adding the negative ions from the  apci  have you investigated if it's from a  reduction in space charging  yeah so i you know again i can only say  what experiments we've done that we've  done this apci experiment  we've also done experiment where we put  the same voltage  on a flat piece of metal so there's no  corona discharge and we do not see this  focusing effect  so i i'm in complete agreement that this  is due to a reduction in space  charge due to the interaction of the  positive and negative ions  and this is something that's been known  um  for actually quite a while irving  langmuir actually  proposed this idea you know way back  so this is something that's known  to my knowledge it's not been done at  these high pressures with this low of  energy ions  so that's what makes this kind of unique  and it's really kind of exciting  okay great next i noticed the  ion introduction was carried out using a  nano spray emitter  how do you expect this to perform with  higher flow ion sources like esi  so generally i would say at you know  first glance i would expect there to be  pretty similar performance with higher  flow rate sources but this does bring up  you know the last point on my last slide  which is that we don't understand what  role droplets versus bare ions play  so with these uh higher flow you know  electrospray sources  you'll be getting larger droplets the  droplets will live longer than the  smaller droplets produced by nano spray  so we're still actually we have some  data looking into this but it's not very  conclusive  i'm looking at how different sized  emitters which give you different size  droplets uh influence focusing or ion  transport in the air  this is still ongoing though so i don't  want to speculate too much but  i would say that you will see focusing  effects whether they're of the same  magnitude or not  remains to be seen okay  and we're going to go ahead and wrap up  with one last question we're back to the  the 3d printing  topic do you have any commentary on the  size of the 3d printed devices  such as what happens as the device size  is either increased or decreased  so this is something we haven't really  messed around with too much  actually i can say that we did a series  of experiments where we  focused an ion beam through just single  apertures with different sized openings  as you would expect as the opening  decreases in size you get a tighter  focus of your ion beam  and to a certain point you actually get  increased  intensity of your ion beam as well  although it drops off  pretty quick once you go to smaller and  smaller apertures  as for these uh complex devices  you know this ims that we've built my  co-worker rob actually printed a version  of it that was half  sized in every dimension and we saw  very similar peak widths to the longer  ones that we've made  so that was kind of an interesting  result  i would expect that you could modify the  focusing  by changing the size of these devices  but something we  are still i guess you know haven't  really looked at yet  okay great well that's all we have today  so before we go i want to thank our  presenters one last time  for all of their work and they're  answering the questions and also thank  the audience for joining us today  and for submitting those questions for  that period  anything that we didn't have time for  today for the q  a and anything that gets submitted  during the on-demand period  will be addressed by the speakers using  the contact information that you  provided  at the time of your registration the  webcast can be viewed on demand through  june of 2021 and until next time hope  you have a great day


